Durarara!! AU
Chapter 29 – Part 2 
Takeshi turned to go with his face still in his hands.
Curtis picked up a rock from the planter like it had been waiting there for punchlines.
He whipped it.
Not a killing throw. A correction with extra physics. It caught Takeshi in the back of the belt, the kind of tap that stole dignity instead of bone.
Takeshi went forward, arms windmilling, pompadour leading, and folded into the municipal trash bin with a sound that would live on someone’s phone by dinner. Bags. Cans. A lid that clapped shut on Elvis.
Youko shrieked.
Curtis rolled his shoulder inside the ripped jacket.
“Bunch of assholes. Always want me to lose my cool first.”
Rio put a hand over her mouth. It did not hide the laugh.
Anri stared at the bin, then at him, torn between you promised a warning and he ripped your jacket.
Takeshi’s legs kicked once over the rim.
“Youko—! Help— it smells—!”
“Get out of there!” She hauled at his waist and got trash on her sleeve for the trouble. “This isn’t over! You hear me? Orihara— the Dollars— you’re dead—”
Curtis was already walking, Anri on one side, Rio on the other, torn Raira blue hanging off him like a bad flag.
“If I’m dead, take a number,” he said without looking back. “The line’s long.”
On a gate that had wanted a brawl,
a rock chose comedy.
Takeshi learned a bin.
Curtis Nightstar kept his spine-rule and lost his cool
only enough to make the trash do the rest.
  Rio and Anri went ahead.
“We’ll wait at the light,” Rio called, already walking like a person who refused to babysit a ripped jacket in a gate crowd. Anri glanced back once. Curtis tipped two fingers. Go. I’m right behind you.
He wasn’t.
A shadow peeled off the wall like it had been born there.
“Fufufu. What a performance, Nightstar-kun. Trash as a punchline. Very Ikebukuro.”
Izaya Orihara stood in the space the girls had just emptied, hands in his pockets, smile already late to the party on purpose. He had not been in the first row. He never needed to be. A phone video and a hobby were enough.
Curtis stopped.
Ripped shoulder. Slapped-hand still warm. Motel still under the uniform. The last person he wanted after I missed you and a bin.
“…You’re in the way.”
“Am I?” Izaya tilted his head, eyes bright. “Your audience went on without you. How rude of them. Or how convenient for a chat.” A glance at the torn Raira blue. “Takeshi and Youko. Hiroshi’s little club. You keep collecting extras. And you keep refusing to break spines. That’s almost a brand.”
Curtis’s jaw ticked.
“Say what you want and move. I’m not doing the gameshow at a school gate.”
Izaya’s smile widened, delighted that the foreigner still used gameshow like a scar they shared.
On a sidewalk the girls had already left, the information broker
filled the gap. Anri and Rio walked toward a light. Izaya Orihara
stopped Curtis Nightstar with a laugh and a ripped jacket
that looked like an opening.
Curtis didn’t bother smiling.
“Or did you learn to pay the price for info on me.”
Izaya’s laugh came out light, almost fond.
“Ah. That price.” He rocked on his heels, looking at the ripped jacket like it was a footnote. “Friendship. You said it like a cashier. As if I keep it in a drawer next to other people’s secrets.” His eyes narrowed, pleased. “I don’t. You already know that. That’s why you named it.”
“Then you get nothing.” Curtis stepped half a pace to go around him. “Move.”
Izaya slid with him, not blocking so much as orbiting.
“I didn’t come to buy you. I came because a video of a pompadour in a bin is already traveling, and people will want a name to put on the hand that threw the rock.” A tilt. “I could give them a kind one. Transfer student. Class rep. Tired boy who missed last period. Or I could give them a more interesting one. The kind that makes Yagiri and color gangs lean in.”
Curtis’s stare went flat.
“That’s not payment. That’s a threat with extra syllables.”
“It’s an offer to be useful.” Izaya’s smile didn’t reach the truth. “You want a friend. I want a specimen who talks. We are stuck at the register.” He flicked two fingers toward the light where Anri and Rio had stopped, looking back. “Your audience is waiting. Tell me one small thing — why a hotel, why a missed class, why you smell like cheap soap that isn’t Raira — and I’ll tell you why Takeshi asked for me at your gate.”
Curtis snorted.
“You smashed Youko’s phone. That’s why. I’m not new.”
Izaya’s grin twitched. Hit.
“See? You don’t need me. That’s what makes you expensive.” A softer, worse beat. “Friendship. What a hideous price. I almost want to learn it just to see the look on your face when I counterfeit it.”
Curtis leaned in just enough that the smile had to hold still.
“Counterfeit doesn’t clear the register. You know where I stand. Info for info if you don’t lie. The rest stays off the table until you can say a name without putting it in a jar.” He straightened. “Now you’re in the way of two people who actually waited.”
On a sidewalk between a bin and a light, the price did not change. Izaya Orihara still could not buy what he wanted.
Curtis Nightstar still would not sell what he was for a smile
that collected humans instead of keeping them.  
Curtis chuckled. It wasn’t kind.
“We’ve been through this, Izaya. I don’t give in to your threats. I don’t play your games. And let a video circulate. It doesn’t bother me.” A small, mean tilt of his head. “Aww. What’s wrong? I’m not your ‘Shizu-chan,’ that I’m just going to let you push my buttons.”
Izaya’s smile froze at the edges.
The nickname in someone else’s mouth always did that — even when it was aimed as a comparison, not a caress.
“Fufu. Careful. You say his name like you know the shape of the hook.” His eyes glittered, offended and delighted in the same breath. “Shizu-chan breaks things. You throw rocks into trash and call it composure. Don’t flatter yourself into his category. You’re still a boy who missed class and tore a jacket over extras.”
“I’m a boy who told you the price and watched you bounce the check.” Curtis stepped past him this time, shoulder to the open sidewalk. “Videos don’t own me. You don’t own me. Push a button that isn’t there and you’ll just look busy.”
Izaya turned with him, coat flaring, voice lighter than his face.
“How dull. A human who won’t flinch for the camera. Fine. Circulate. I do love an honest specimen.” A pause, almost private. “But the day you do break a spine, Nightstar-kun — I’ll be there to name it. That’s not a threat. That’s a calendar.”
Curtis didn’t look back.
“Put it next to friendship. You don’t have that date either.”
At the light, Rio lifted her chin. Anri’s eyes found him, then the man in the fur-trimmed coat, and understood enough to hate the timing.
On a sidewalk that wanted a reaction, Shizu-chan did not land. Izaya Orihara got a chuckle instead of a riot.
Curtis Nightstar walked toward two girls who had waited and left the broker holding a button
that wouldn’t press.
Curtis laughed under his breath, already done with the register.
“Anyways. Izaya. Why are you here.”
Izaya’s smile found its real target.
“For him, of course.”
Curtis looked behind him.
Mikado Ryuugamine stood a few meters back like the city had spit him out at the worst light. Bag. First-year face. The look of a boy who had followed a rumor and found a ripped jacket and a flea-market coat instead of a quiet walk home.
Curtis’s mind went cold and simple.
Mikado, what the fuck are you doing? You’re supposed to head back to your place for Mika…. you fucking idiot. Mika better be okay.
Nothing of that reached his mouth.
“Yo, Mikado!”
Easy. Public. A class-rep wave with a torn shoulder.
Mikado startled, then flushed, caught between Anri at the light, Rio beside her, Izaya’s delighted stare, and Curtis saying his name like they were friends instead of a jealousy he hadn’t unpacked.
“C-Curtis-kun. I— I was just—” His eyes flicked to Izaya and away, the warning Masaomi had given him sitting visible in his throat. “I heard there was a commotion at the gate.”
Izaya clasped his hands, radiant.
“See? He comes when the city makes noise. Isn’t he precious.”
Curtis stepped half a pace so he was between the broker and the first-year without making a show of it.
“Noise is over. Pompadour learned a bin. You can go home.” A beat, aimed at Mikado, light enough to be ordinary. “You are going home, right? Not collecting extra problems before dinner.”
Mikado swallowed.
Anri had already turned fully around. Rio’s eyes narrowed at Izaya like she could smell the hobby.
On a sidewalk that had too many names,
the real appointment arrived.
Izaya Orihara had come for Mikado.
Curtis Nightstar said yo
and kept a kettle
and a scarred girl
off the record —
while calling a first-year
an idiot
only in his head.  
